/**

 @page page_exahype_runtime_analysis Runtime analysis

 ExaHyPE 2 ships a small set of Python postprocessing utilities to analyse a
 run's output. They live in the top-level @c postprocessing package and
 are plain command line scripts. Every script accepts @c --help for a full list
 of options.


 ## Error norms and probe data

 If your solver writes error norms or probe traces to a CSV file, you can turn
 them into matplotlib figures:

 <code>
 python3 postprocessing/plot_error_norms.py errors.csv --output errors.png
 python3 postprocessing/plot_probe_data.py probe.csv --output probe.png
 </code>

 Both scripts write a PNG (the @c --output path defaults to the input file name
 with a @c .png suffix). @c plot_error_norms.py reads the L1, L2 and L_inf error
 columns and plots them over the simulation time, while @c plot_probe_data.py
 plots the recorded quantities at a probe location as a time series.


 ## Rendering and comparing solutions

 To render solution snapshots (VTKHDF, HDF5/XMF, NetCDF or VTU) into images, or to
 compare two solution files and report the first mismatch, use:

 <code>
 python3 postprocessing/render_solution.py MyProject.output --output-dir frames
 python3 postprocessing/compare_solution.py reference.vtu candidate.vtu
 </code>


 ## Peano data

 As ExaHyPE 2 is built on top of Peano 4, all the generic Peano runtime
 analysis scripts and @ref page_peano_runtime_analysis "Peano's statistics"
 work for ExaHyPE, too.


*/
